00:38:36.960 --> 00:38:42.960
sort algorithm uses bounding box prediction,&nbsp;
Kalman filters and IOU matching techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:49.520
First let us start with bounding box predictions.&nbsp;
The first step in this algorithm is to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:54.800
bounding boxes or detect where are the&nbsp;
objects present in the image. Now, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.800 --> 00:39:01.440
can be accomplished using any CNN architecture it&nbsp;
can be YOLO it can be our CNN or it can even be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:08.880
simple computer vision handcrafted model. For our&nbsp;
project we have used the Yolo v3 algorithm briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:14.560
what you know v3 algorithm does given an input&nbsp;
image it is going to detect all the objects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:20.560
are present in that image, draw a bounding box&nbsp;
around them, and also tell us what that object is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:26.160
After we generated the bounding box predictions&nbsp;
we get to the next step, which is the Kalman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:31.760
filter. Now Kalman filter is a very broad topic,&nbsp;
which would take an entire video to explain, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:37.680
still we would be left with a lot of ground&nbsp;
to cover. So to briefly discuss Kalman filter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:43.440
we can say in very simple terms that Kalman&nbsp;
filter is a linear approximation. So what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:49.040
the role of kalman filter over here? Kalman&nbsp;
filter needs to predict what is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:54.480
the future location of a given detected&nbsp;
object. Using these future predictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:59.600
we can determine whether the object that we&nbsp;
were tracking isn't the same object or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:07.200
Along with that, using prediction, we can&nbsp;
also deal with the problem of occlusion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:12.480
So what is occlusion? In this example above,&nbsp;
we can see between these these two frames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:13.360 --> 00:40:19.920
the two balls are going to overlap each other. And&nbsp;
whichever object is in front is going to occlude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:25.760
the object that is present behind it. In order&nbsp;
to deal with this problem in object tracking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:25.760 --> 00:40:33.280
predictions are useful. So how does Kalman filter&nbsp;
works? Kalman filter assumes a linear velocity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:38.960
model and generates the predictions according to&nbsp;
it. Once we get the real data where the object is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:44.960
we input that again to the Kalman filter. The&nbsp;
Kalman filter improves its predictions based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:52.000
the real data that it got, and again generates a&nbsp;
new set of predictions. In this way Kalman filter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:58.320
works in an iterative manner and keeps improving&nbsp;
its predictions. As an output, the Kalman filter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:04.240
does not output a single number, actually, it&nbsp;
outputs a probability distribution of where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:11.040
object can be in a given set of locations. If we&nbsp;
take the maximum value of that probability, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:16.160
in some way approximate where the object is going&nbsp;
to be. This is the work of the Kalman filter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:16.160 --> 00:41:24.000
it generates future predictions for the objects.&nbsp;
The next step we have is called IO U matching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.840 --> 00:41:32.720
Io U stands for intersection over Union. In brief&nbsp;
terms, we can say IOU gives us a quantitative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:38.960
score to determine how much two bounding boxes&nbsp;
are similar to each other based on their location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:45.040
in the image as well as the size, but is this&nbsp;
IOU matching in the context of sort algorithm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.040 --> 00:41:50.080
let us consider this case, we have n different&nbsp;
cards that we are detecting in a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:56.560
frame. And we have n IDs that we need to assign&nbsp;
to these particular cards, like in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.560 --> 00:42:03.280
IDs are useful to determine which object we are&nbsp;
tracking. So we need to assign these n detections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:03.280 --> 00:42:11.840
to these n IDs. For each of the n IDs, we have&nbsp;
IOU score corresponding to each detection in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:18.320
descriptive way, we have the detected objects&nbsp;
in the row, and we have the IDs in the columns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:18.320 --> 00:42:25.680
So for each of the ID and the GS detection, we&nbsp;
have an IOU score. Now we want to assign the IOU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:32.640
scores to these n detections in such a way that&nbsp;
the total IOU score is maximized. solving this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:38.480
problem using brute force approach takes order&nbsp;
complexity n factorial, which is very large.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:44.080
In order to solve this, the sort algorithm uses&nbsp;
the Hungarian algorithm, the Hungarian algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.080 --> 00:42:52.400
solves this linear assignment problem in order&nbsp;
complexity n cubed, which is a great improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.400 --> 00:42:59.040
over the n factorial algorithm. Once we have&nbsp;
assigned these n predictions to N IDs, we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:05.200
in a sense solved the tracking problem for the AI&nbsp;
X frame, then we can run this algorithm again in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:12.000
a loop and again, keep track of the objects that&nbsp;
are in the next frame of the video. So this is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:17.920
the simple salt algorithm works. Not deep salt&nbsp;
is an extension of the simple sort algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:23.120
Now that deep in the deep salt comes from this&nbsp;
step called the deep appearance descriptor step.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.720 --> 00:43:29.920
Along with that, we also have a cascade matching&nbsp;
step that is added to the simple sort algorithm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:29.920 --> 00:43:36.000
So deep appearance descriptor is a convolutional&nbsp;
neural network, which is trained to detect a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:36.000 --> 00:43:43.440
similar object in different images. What this&nbsp;
means this, this network can tell given a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:49.920
of images of the same person in different views,&nbsp;
whether it is the same person or not. So as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:49.920 --> 00:43:56.480
input, the deep descriptor receives a cropped&nbsp;
image of the object detected. And as an output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.480 --> 00:44:03.120
we try to receive a vector that encodes the&nbsp;
information that is present in that crowd image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.120 --> 00:44:10.560
these encoded vectors would allow us to compare&nbsp;
different objects. So now we have two models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.560 --> 00:44:15.600
that are describing us about similar&nbsp;
objects in different frames of a video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:22.080
we combined the score given by these two models in&nbsp;
a linear fashion. The score by the deep descriptor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.080 --> 00:44:28.000
is given using the cosine distance metric, and&nbsp;
the score by the Kalman filter is given by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:33.360
melanomas distance before discussing how these&nbsp;
metrics are computed. Let us first discuss the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:40.000
notion of a distance metric. A distance metric&nbsp;
is a score that we assign to two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:47.440
entities A and B to tell how similar they are.&nbsp;
For instance, if we consider it to deep plane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:55.440
on that plane, we have two points A and B.&nbsp;
We compute the distance from the origin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.320 --> 00:45:02.240
If the two entities A and B are close to each&nbsp;
other then distance from the origin is also going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.240 --> 00:45:08.320
to be similar in value. If the two entities&nbsp;
A and B are very dissimilar to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:13.360
then these two entities are going to have a&nbsp;
Euclidean distance that is very dissimilar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.360 --> 00:45:18.320
we can say one is going to have a negative value,&nbsp;
the other one is going to have a positive value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.320 --> 00:45:25.120
if they are lying on the two sides of the Y axis.&nbsp;
In these two cases, we need to use another notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.120 --> 00:45:30.960
of distance metric. For the deep descriptors, we&nbsp;
use the cosine distance metric, how do we compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.960 --> 00:45:41.120
this? If we are given two entities A and B. And&nbsp;
from the origin, we draw a vector joining these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:43.360 --> 00:45:50.080
and then we calculate the angle between these two&nbsp;
vectors, the cosine value of this angle is going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:56.160
to give us the cosine distance metric. Let us&nbsp;
consider an example. If we have two vectors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.160 --> 00:46:02.320
are overlapping each other, the angle between them&nbsp;
is going to be zero, and the cosine value of zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:02.320 --> 00:46:06.480
is going to be one. This means that these&nbsp;
two vectors are very similar to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:12.640
However, if two vectors are perpendicular to each&nbsp;
other, the cosine value of 90 is going to be zero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:13.280 --> 00:46:18.720
This would mean that these two vectors are very&nbsp;
dissimilar to each other. In this way, we would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:24.160
able to determine whether the two objects that we&nbsp;
detected how much similar they are to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:24.720 --> 00:46:30.240
For the Kalman filter case, we are not using the&nbsp;
cosine similarity. A reason for this is the Kalman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:36.400
filter is going to output not a single point, but&nbsp;
a probability distribution. In order to compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:42.400
the similarity between a point and a probability&nbsp;
distribution, we use melanomas distance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:50.640
If we consider that this is the distribution that&nbsp;
is output by the Kalman filter, this implies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:57.280
the probability of the object being over here&nbsp;
is very high compared to the values outwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:58.960 --> 00:47:03.520
And this is the point that we want to&nbsp;
compare with this probability distribution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:10.160
In very simple terms, what melanomas distance&nbsp;
does, for a 2d version, we compute the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:15.440
directions in which the data spread is the&nbsp;
highest. For this case, we can see the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:21.520
spread is the highest in this direction and a&nbsp;
perpendicular this direction. Then we normalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:28.080
and transform the coordinate axes in such a way&nbsp;
that these two axes become the coordinate axes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:34.720
In that transformed space, we calculate&nbsp;
the distance of this point from the origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:42.320
a value of that distance is the melanomas&nbsp;
distance. Using this metric, we decide how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:49.120
a given prediction by the Kalman filter matches an&nbsp;
object that we just detected. Combining these two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:55.280
we get an overall scalar score value that&nbsp;
we can use to assign detections to their ids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.280 --> 00:48:01.120
using the N Galeon algorithm. However, in&nbsp;
the implementation, there is a slight catch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:01.120 --> 00:48:07.520
the authors of the deep sort algorithm noticed&nbsp;
that the predictions by the Kalman filter are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:12.480
very useful, and the score for these predictions&nbsp;
can be neglected. However, you will see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:18.480
in our project, this doesn't work very well. The&nbsp;
next step that we have is cascade matching. Gaskin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:24.480
matching is an extension to the IOU matching&nbsp;
algorithm for the assignment problem, the cascade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:24.480 --> 00:48:30.560
matching takes into account the temporal dimension&nbsp;
as well, in order to reduce the time complexity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:36.880
for the Hungarian algorithm cascade matching tries&nbsp;
and match the latest detections with the latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.880 --> 00:48:44.800
IDs and the later or old detections with the&nbsp;
old IDs. All of this process combined gives us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:44.800 --> 00:48:53.280
the deep salt algorithm. And we can again run this&nbsp;
algorithm in a loop to track objects in a complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

