00:54:41.280 --> 00:54:48.480
In a general self driving car setting. We&nbsp;
usually have two main sensors which are Lidar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:48.480 --> 00:54:56.640
and the camera. Typically, these Lidar and&nbsp;
camera sensors are not going to be placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:02.240
at the same location. They are going to have&nbsp;
different locations turns on a car. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.240 --> 00:55:08.160
in this case, the Lidar is on top of the car,&nbsp;
and the camera is on the front head of the car.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.520 --> 00:55:16.320
Camera, as we all know, is a visual sensor,&nbsp;
we use it to capture a 2d scene of a given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:22.080
3d environment. Lidar, on the other hand&nbsp;
is a perception sensor. It is used to map&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:29.840
a 3d environment using a rotating laser. LIDAR&nbsp;
generates what are called the point clouds. Point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:29.840 --> 00:55:36.720
Clouds provide a very good representation of a&nbsp;
given 3d environment. Now, this is a very general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:36.720 --> 00:55:45.200
use case that we want to see the data that was&nbsp;
captured by a lidar in the frame of a camera. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.200 --> 00:55:52.080
I mean by that, if through our eyes, we are seeing&nbsp;
how the environment is looking like from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.640 --> 00:55:58.960
we also need to see how the same environment at&nbsp;
the same time is going to look like when we see it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.960 --> 00:56:05.760
from over here at the camera location. In order to&nbsp;
convert between these given reference points, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:12.000
use what are called homogeneous transformations.&nbsp;
If we consider that we are given two coordinate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:19.200
axes, the blue one over here, and the red one over&nbsp;
here, and we have this object, this black dot that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:26.320
we want to see through both of these coordinate&nbsp;
axes. Mathematically, this problem translates to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:27.760 --> 00:56:33.760
if we are given the vector from the origin of&nbsp;
this coordinate axis to this point over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:40.640
which we call x, we need to find x dash, which&nbsp;
is a vector starting from the origin of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:48.240
coordinate axis to the same point given this&nbsp;
coordinate axis is called transformation. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:54.720
any general transformation can be divided into two&nbsp;
subproblems, which are translation and rotation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:55.440 --> 00:57:02.320
Translation is simply moving the origin of this&nbsp;
coordinate axis to some other location in space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:08.240
If we have a coordinate axis like this,&nbsp;
and we simply move this to a new location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:08.240 --> 00:57:10.160
then we have what is called a translation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.360 --> 00:57:17.280
In rotation, however, we would like to change&nbsp;
the direction in which these axes are pointing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:18.080 --> 00:57:24.480
However, the origin is going to remain at the same&nbsp;
place. In order to understand rotation better, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:24.480 --> 00:57:30.800
can divide the rotation operation into three sub&nbsp;
operations, which are rotation about the x axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:37.040
rotation about the y axis and rotation about&nbsp;
the z axis. A combination of all these three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:37.040 --> 00:57:41.600
rotations gives us the actual rotation&nbsp;
that is going to finally take place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:42.640 --> 00:57:47.840
Without going into much depth, let us see how the&nbsp;
mathematical representations of these look like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:48.800 --> 00:57:54.800
In order to get the translated vector, we simply&nbsp;
need to add another vector to our original vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:01.360
In this case, we are adding this ABC vector&nbsp;
to our original x to get x t, which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.360 --> 00:58:08.640
translated vector, the rotation operation can&nbsp;
be understood as a matrix multiplication. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:14.400
we consider this three cross three matrix, and we&nbsp;
multiply this matrix with our original vector x,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:14.400 --> 00:58:21.920
we get a rotated vector XR, however, we need to&nbsp;
keep this in mind, the values of this translation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.920 --> 00:58:30.240
vector can be taken anything, but the parameters&nbsp;
of this matrix are bounded between a range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:31.520 --> 00:58:37.440
combining these two sub operations of translation&nbsp;
and rotation, we get what is called a homogeneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:37.440 --> 00:58:44.720
transformation. What homogeneous transformation&nbsp;
does, it simply reduces translation and rotation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:44.720 --> 00:58:52.160
into a single matrix multiplication, as we can&nbsp;
see over here. So how we derive this matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:58.880
multiplier, first up, we take the rotation matrix&nbsp;
and place it in the upper left corner over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:58.880 --> 00:59:03.440
we take this translation vector, we place&nbsp;
it in the upper right corner over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:03.440 --> 00:59:10.640
and these remaining part, this last row is&nbsp;
filled with three zeros and a one. This will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:10.640 --> 00:59:15.600
look something like this, this three cross&nbsp;
three matrix is the rotation matrix. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.600 --> 00:59:21.680
this three dimensional vector is the translation&nbsp;
vector, rest zeros and ones are placed over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.480 --> 00:59:28.960
Now following the rules of matrix multiplication,&nbsp;
given this is a full Cross for matrix, it can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.960 --> 00:59:36.160
be multiplied with a vector of dimensionality for&nbsp;
in order to make this three dimensional vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:36.160 --> 00:59:41.200
a four dimensional vector, what we&nbsp;
do is place a one over here. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:41.200 --> 00:59:46.320
our complete operation looks like this. We'll&nbsp;
take this matrix, we multiply this matrix with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:46.320 --> 00:59:51.440
this extended vector. And what we get as an&nbsp;
output is this extended vector over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.080 --> 00:59:58.960
because of the structure of this matrix over here,&nbsp;
this one over here is a guaranteed number. Using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:58.960 --> 01:00:06.240
this operation, we Finally get a vector x dash&nbsp;
representing the point x in a new frame of access.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:14.080
Now, in order to convert this given 3d data&nbsp;
into a 2d representative screen, just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:14.080 --> 01:00:19.360
we have in a camera, we use the operations that&nbsp;
we will discuss over here. Now mathematically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:25.280
how does a camera work? If we consider this&nbsp;
red coordinate axis as the camera axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:30.400
we have this point in the 3d scene that&nbsp;
we can see through this camera axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:31.600 --> 01:00:37.600
Now we define a new point which is called the&nbsp;
camera Center, which is taken to be the origin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:37.600 --> 01:00:44.320
of this coordinate axis over here. Now all the&nbsp;
points that are situated at a distance of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:44.320 --> 01:00:50.800
unit from this camera center in the direction&nbsp;
of z axis, is what we call the image plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:52.000 --> 01:00:58.640
Now this image plane is a plane to the surface,&nbsp;
which has defined its x&amp;y coordinates in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:05.200
direction, the x direction is taken upward and y&nbsp;
direction is taken along this direction. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.200 --> 01:01:12.320
also understand this image plane as sitting at&nbsp;
the camera center and watching a cinema screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:18.960
that is one unit away from us. Now&nbsp;
this complete problem boils down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:20.720 --> 01:01:27.520
if we are given this 3d point, we want&nbsp;
to project this 3d point to this image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:29.040 --> 01:01:36.800
That is, how would this 3d Point look like if&nbsp;
we were to see it in a 2d projected image plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:37.680 --> 01:01:45.600
And this is exactly what a camera does converts a&nbsp;
3d scene into a 2d image. We accomplish this using&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:45.600 --> 01:01:52.240
the similar homogeneous transformations. If we&nbsp;
are given a transformation matrix M, we can simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.240 --> 01:02:00.400
multiply that with a 3d object vector x dash and&nbsp;
get a new vector, which is x double dash. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:00.400 --> 01:02:07.920
in order to get how these points would look like&nbsp;
in this image plane, we need to divide the X and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:15.120
Y coordinates of this newly x double dash vector&nbsp;
with its Z coordinate, which we can see over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:17.120 --> 01:02:23.760
Solving both of these operations, we would get the&nbsp;
transformation of a 3d point on a 2d image plane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.720 --> 01:02:30.880
In our case, how that works out is we are given&nbsp;
the point cloud that is generated by the LIDAR&nbsp;&nbsp;

