01:10:32.160 --> 01:10:36.160
Now, let us have a look at the technique&nbsp;
that we are going to use to solve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.160 --> 01:10:42.640
multitask learning problem. Alright, so the&nbsp;
problem of multitask learning is as follows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.520 --> 01:10:48.560
We want to derive a network that can&nbsp;
be used to solve different tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:48.560 --> 01:10:54.720
these different tasks can be object detection,&nbsp;
object classification, and Object segmentation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:55.280 --> 01:11:01.120
The constraint in this problem is that we cannot&nbsp;
have different networks for these different tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:08.320
we have to use a single network that is run once&nbsp;
and trained once to solve all the different tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.320 --> 01:11:14.880
The advantage it has over distributed networks is&nbsp;
that we don't have to train each and every network&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:20.560
individually for the different tasks resulting&nbsp;
in saving some time. So the technique that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:20.560 --> 01:11:28.560
going to discuss for this multitask learning&nbsp;
is m 10. M 10 stands for multi task attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:28.560 --> 01:11:36.000
network. And this is the architecture that M tan&nbsp;
follows. Given an input image, the first step we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:44.560
do is calculate the shared features of that given&nbsp;
input image. Then we have task specific modules&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:52.400
that use those shared features to solve their&nbsp;
given task. For n number of tasks, we are going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:52.400 --> 01:11:58.640
have n specific attention modules. These attention&nbsp;
modules take some data from the shared features&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:59.280 --> 01:12:05.760
and augment it in such a way that is useful&nbsp;
for their particular task. The word attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:05.760 --> 01:12:10.960
over here refers to the attention that&nbsp;
each module gives do its particular task.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.920 --> 01:12:17.760
For the intent technique, we can use any&nbsp;
convolutional network to calculate the shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:17.760 --> 01:12:23.440
features for our particular project, we are going&nbsp;
to use set net to calculate the shared features.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.160 --> 01:12:30.240
Now signal is a network that is used to solve the&nbsp;
image segmentation task in the image segmentation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:36.320
task, we would be given an input image and the&nbsp;
task is to segment the different objects that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:36.320 --> 01:12:42.320
present in that image. For this image segmentation&nbsp;
task, the networks that are used have a general&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.320 --> 01:12:48.000
structure and general structure is such that we&nbsp;
are given an input image the first step we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:53.600
is downsample the image and derive the features&nbsp;
from that image once we downsample the image and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:59.200
reach the bottleneck, we have sample the image&nbsp;
to the same dimension as the given input image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.200 --> 01:13:03.200
And with the help of the features&nbsp;
derived in the downsampling task&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:03.200 --> 01:13:09.520
output a segmentation mask. The downsampling&nbsp;
part of the network is also called the encoder&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.520 --> 01:13:15.840
and the upsampling part of the network is also&nbsp;
called the decoder. The only difference between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:15.840 --> 01:13:21.760
Cygnet and fully convolutional neural networks&nbsp;
is the operation that we use for upsampling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:22.480 --> 01:13:26.640
So the Signet architecture has different&nbsp;
convolution blocks. As you can see over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.480 --> 01:13:34.640
these convolution blocks comprise of convolution&nbsp;
layers followed by some pooling layers. Now, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.640 --> 01:13:40.880
M 10 architecture is in such a way that for each&nbsp;
of these convolution blocks, we have attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.880 --> 01:13:47.040
some modules, these sub modules are for the blue&nbsp;
tasks and these sub modules are for the red task.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.320 --> 01:13:54.400
The arrows over here show the data flow that is&nbsp;
happening between the overall network. Now there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:54.400 --> 01:13:59.840
are some differences in the upsampling attention&nbsp;
modules and the downsampling attention modules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:01.520 --> 01:14:07.360
Let us discuss them in slight detail. For&nbsp;
the upsample attention module, we get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:07.360 --> 01:14:12.960
input from the previous attention module which&nbsp;
we can see over here. And we take another input&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:17.440
that is derived from the first layer of the&nbsp;
convolutional block as we can see over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.840 --> 01:14:25.680
these two matrices are merged the convolution&nbsp;
operation on them is computed twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.280 --> 01:14:32.720
and then we calculate the element wise&nbsp;
multiplication of the output generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.720 --> 01:14:39.040
over here and the input of one of the filters of&nbsp;
these convolution blocks as we can see over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:45.440
then this element wise multiplication&nbsp;
is once again sent through a convolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:45.440 --> 01:14:51.680
block and the output is returned to the next&nbsp;
attention module. For the downsampling module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:51.680 --> 01:14:58.000
we we take the input from the previous&nbsp;
attention module, which we can see over here&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:00.960 --> 01:15:06.480
This input is sent through a convolution&nbsp;
block which is then merged with the input&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:06.480 --> 01:15:09.840
of the first layer of the convolution&nbsp;
block which is present over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.720 --> 01:15:16.240
This merge filter is passed&nbsp;
twice through convolution blocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:17.360 --> 01:15:21.680
then an element wise multiplication&nbsp;
is calculated for this output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:21.680 --> 01:15:26.720
and the input that is taken from one of the layers&nbsp;
of convolution block as we can see over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:28.800 --> 01:15:32.400
And this final output is sent&nbsp;
to the next attention module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:37.680
the attention module is only different for the&nbsp;
up sampling and down sampling phase of the shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:37.680 --> 01:15:44.000
feature network. For the different tasks, the&nbsp;
attention module architecture is exactly the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.280 --> 01:15:52.080
And this is the complete network architecture of&nbsp;
the M 10 network. One additional technique that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:52.080 --> 01:15:59.840
M 10 proposes is what is called dynamic weighted&nbsp;
averaging. For any multi task learning network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:00.480 --> 01:16:06.880
we calculate the loss by a weighted average of&nbsp;
the loss calculated for the different tasks as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:06.880 --> 01:16:12.080
you can see over here, the adjustment&nbsp;
of the weights of these losses is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:12.080 --> 01:16:16.000
important for the network to actually&nbsp;
converge and learn the different tasks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:22.640
Dynamic weight averaging is a technique&nbsp;
that is used to determine these weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.280 --> 01:16:30.000
based on the rate of change of the gradient loss.&nbsp;
Now, what is the rate of change of gradient loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:35.600
if we consider two training batches of images&nbsp;
input to the network at time t and time t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.600 --> 01:16:43.680
plus one the difference between the loss that is&nbsp;
back propagated at time t plus one and time t is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:50.320
what is called the rate of change or gradient&nbsp;
loss, we calculate these weight values by taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:50.320 --> 01:16:55.920
a softmax kind of average using the rate of&nbsp;
change of loss. And this technique gives us&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.920 --> 01:17:00.000
the optimal value of the weights that we&nbsp;
should have for these individual losses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:00.640 --> 01:17:05.120
So, these are the two main contributions of&nbsp;
the multitask attention network technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

