01:24:41.520 --> 01:24:46.640
Let us discuss these techniques one by one.&nbsp;
The first component that we're going to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:46.640 --> 01:24:55.040
is the key point SPN before discussing key&nbsp;
point FPN let us first discuss what is FPN SBN&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:55.040 --> 01:25:01.760
stands for feature pyramid network feature pyramid&nbsp;
network is a feature feature extraction technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:01.760 --> 01:25:08.640
that given a single input image outputs&nbsp;
different feature maps that vary in size.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:09.200 --> 01:25:14.800
It's something as you can see over here, given&nbsp;
a single input image, the outputs are different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:14.800 --> 01:25:21.280
feature maps of different sizes. So what is&nbsp;
feature extraction and feature maps. All the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:21.280 --> 01:25:28.800
important data that is contained inside an image&nbsp;
is what are called features. They can be points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:28.800 --> 01:25:36.400
edges, designs and patterns or even human faces.&nbsp;
In a convolutional neural network, we have what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:36.400 --> 01:25:42.640
are called convolution blocks. These convolution&nbsp;
blocks consist of different convolution layers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:42.640 --> 01:25:50.160
and pooling layers. We provide an input image to&nbsp;
these convolution blocks, and we get an output&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:56.000
image or we can say a matrix. This output image&nbsp;
or metrics are what are called feature maps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.000 --> 01:26:02.400
The process of extracting features out of a given&nbsp;
image is what is called Feature Extraction. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:02.400 --> 01:26:09.360
feature pyramid networks is not a network by name,&nbsp;
it's actually a general technique that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:09.360 --> 01:26:16.480
employ on a number of different neural networks.&nbsp;
For instance, we can apply this feature pyramid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:16.480 --> 01:26:23.360
network technique to generate feature maps on a&nbsp;
ResNet architecture as well. By post processing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:23.360 --> 01:26:29.920
these output feature maps, we can do a number of&nbsp;
different tasks like object detection, the feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:29.920 --> 01:26:37.280
pyramid network technique works in this way. Given&nbsp;
this input image, we pass it through a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:37.280 --> 01:26:45.520
different convolution blocks, and ensure that&nbsp;
these images get smaller in size. As we can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:45.520 --> 01:26:51.440
over here, the input image is getting smaller&nbsp;
in size as we approach the tip of the pyramid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:52.640 --> 01:26:58.800
Then we employ successive iterations of&nbsp;
convolutional blocks, ensuring that they up sample&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:58.800 --> 01:27:06.720
the given image as we can see over here, the image&nbsp;
gets larger in size. As we approach the base of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:06.720 --> 01:27:13.200
the pyramid network, along with the convolutions&nbsp;
that we apply in this part of the network. We also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:16.560 --> 01:27:21.200
provide information to this part of the&nbsp;
network from the previous part of the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:21.200 --> 01:27:23.680
as we can see through the&nbsp;
skip connections over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.440 --> 01:27:30.480
and we generate the output feature maps from&nbsp;
this part of the network as we can see over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:31.680 --> 01:27:36.800
Now, feature pyramid networks are a great&nbsp;
technique to employ if we want to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:36.800 --> 01:27:45.200
networks that are scale invariant. So what is&nbsp;
scale invariant network? Let us assume that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:45.200 --> 01:27:51.600
want to predict a ball that is present on an&nbsp;
image using a simple convolutional network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:51.600 --> 01:27:57.760
If the ball that is present in that image&nbsp;
of large size or that ball is of small size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:57.760 --> 01:28:03.520
the neural network should be able to detect&nbsp;
the ball that is present in that image. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.520 --> 01:28:10.560
property of detecting irrespective of the size of&nbsp;
that object is what is called scale invariance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.280 --> 01:28:17.840
for tasks like object detection, we need to&nbsp;
predict a bounding box that tells us where that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:17.840 --> 01:28:24.560
object is present in that image. For tasks like&nbsp;
these scaling variants is an important property&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:24.560 --> 01:28:31.520
that neural networks are supposed to have.&nbsp;
However, for detecting points in a given image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:31.520 --> 01:28:38.400
scaling variants does not play a major role. If&nbsp;
we want to detect a single point in a given image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:38.400 --> 01:28:44.240
that point is going to remain at the same&nbsp;
location, irrespective of the scaling that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:44.240 --> 01:28:51.040
we apply on that image. Hence, directly applying&nbsp;
feature pyramid networks to detect points in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:51.040 --> 01:28:56.960
image is not an efficient technique. Key Point&nbsp;
feature pyramid networks are an extension to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:56.960 --> 01:29:04.480
the given SPM networks that help us detect points&nbsp;
in a given image. The overall architecture of key&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.480 --> 01:29:12.080
point FPN remains the same, the outputs of the SPN&nbsp;
can be visualized, as we can see over here. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:12.080 --> 01:29:19.440
Gaussian shaped outputs that we see over here&nbsp;
indicate that the network is really confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:19.440 --> 01:29:25.840
that a point is present at a given location in&nbsp;
the image. But as we move away from that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:25.840 --> 01:29:30.800
the confidence level of the network goes&nbsp;
down, as we can see from the shape over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.120 --> 01:29:38.160
So once we have these feature output&nbsp;
maps, we convert these feature maps to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:38.160 --> 01:29:44.160
a similar size. In this particular case,&nbsp;
we have taken the reference size to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:44.160 --> 01:29:50.800
largest feature map that is present with us.&nbsp;
We upsample the smaller feature maps to match&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:50.800 --> 01:29:55.440
the size of the largest feature map that we&nbsp;
have, as we can see in this column over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:57.360 --> 01:30:03.680
Then we calculate a weighted average of This&nbsp;
feature maps and get a final single output as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:03.680 --> 01:30:10.640
we can see over here, the weights of the weighted&nbsp;
average are dependent on the confidence value that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:10.640 --> 01:30:17.520
the network has in this different feature maps.&nbsp;
From this feature map, we can take the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:17.520 --> 01:30:23.200
has the highest confidence value and output&nbsp;
that as the point that we wanted to detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:24.000 --> 01:30:30.320
this complete post processing that we have done&nbsp;
after the simple SPM network is what constitutes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:30.320 --> 01:30:37.360
the key point FPN network. Alright, so this is&nbsp;
the complete network architecture of the SFA 3d&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:37.360 --> 01:30:42.560
techniques. Now let us discuss the different&nbsp;
loss functions that are employed to train&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.560 --> 01:30:48.960
this network. First off, we are going to discuss&nbsp;
the outputs that are generated by this network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.600 --> 01:30:55.600
The first output is a heat map of the different&nbsp;
classes. Heat Map is a data visualization&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.600 --> 01:31:02.880
technique that tells us the magnitude of a given&nbsp;
phenomena occurring on a given input. Roughly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:02.880 --> 01:31:09.200
we can visualize a heat map using a mountain&nbsp;
terrain. Usually in a mountainous range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:09.200 --> 01:31:15.200
we have a mountain that has the highest peak,&nbsp;
and has accompanying hills or mountains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.200 --> 01:31:21.760
are off smaller peaks, then this highest peak&nbsp;
Mountain. As we move away from this highest peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:21.760 --> 01:31:27.120
the height of the ground also starts decreasing&nbsp;
in all the directions something like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:29.600 --> 01:31:35.120
As we move away from that highest peak, we&nbsp;
approach another neighboring mountain or a hill.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:35.840 --> 01:31:40.560
After the height decreases, the height&nbsp;
again starts increasing until we reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:40.560 --> 01:31:47.520
the top or the peak of the neighboring mountain.&nbsp;
And once again, as we move away from that peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:47.520 --> 01:31:54.080
the height again starts decreasing. If we view&nbsp;
such a mountainous region from the top view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:54.880 --> 01:32:00.240
and visualize the height of the ground in&nbsp;
that image, we get what is called a heat map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:00.960 --> 01:32:07.600
If we denote a larger height by a dark red&nbsp;
color, and a lower height by a white color,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:08.400 --> 01:32:12.880
we are going to observe that the&nbsp;
highest peak has a solid red color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:12.880 --> 01:32:20.960
As we move away from that peak, that solid red&nbsp;
color is going to decay into a white color. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:20.960 --> 01:32:27.680
as we approach another peak, that white color is&nbsp;
going to converge into a solid red color. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:28.560 --> 01:32:34.720
a heat map can also be visualized in terms&nbsp;
of confidence of a network that it has on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:34.720 --> 01:32:42.240
its predictions. In our particular case, if we&nbsp;
want to detect three objects, let's say a car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.240 --> 01:32:48.960
a truck and a pedestrian, we are going to&nbsp;
generate three different heat maps. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.960 --> 01:32:55.440
the red color denotes that there is a car that is&nbsp;
present in that image at this particular location,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:55.440 --> 01:33:01.760
and the network is confident about this location.&nbsp;
Similarly, a pedestrian is present in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:01.760 --> 01:33:07.840
location, and the network is confident about&nbsp;
its prediction in this particular location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.400 --> 01:33:13.200
As we move away from both these locations,&nbsp;
the confidence of the network goes down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:14.480 --> 01:33:20.240
Using this heat map, we detect the class of&nbsp;
the object whether it is a car or a pedestrian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:21.120 --> 01:33:27.360
In order to encourage the network to learn the&nbsp;
right classes, we use what is called a focal loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:29.600 --> 01:33:33.040
The mathematical expression of the&nbsp;
focal loss is something like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:34.160 --> 01:33:39.520
Focal loss is usually used when there&nbsp;
exists a class imbalance in the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:40.080 --> 01:33:45.200
class imbalance usually occurs when there is&nbsp;
a less amount of data for a particular class&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.200 --> 01:33:48.960
and a very high amount of data&nbsp;
for another particular class.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:49.600 --> 01:33:56.160
Here P T is the confidence value of the network.&nbsp;
This scaling factor over here allows the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:56.160 --> 01:34:03.680
to focus its learning more on the predictions that&nbsp;
it is less confident about and focus less on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:03.680 --> 01:34:12.080
predictions that it is more confident about. Next&nbsp;
output we have are the distance from the ego car&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:12.080 --> 01:34:16.880
and the direction in which the other&nbsp;
objects are facing. In order to learn this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:16.880 --> 01:34:22.160
we use what is called an L one loss. The&nbsp;
mathematical expression is something like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:23.840 --> 01:34:30.320
elven loss simply takes the difference of the&nbsp;
prediction and the actual value and computes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:30.320 --> 01:34:37.120
absolute value of that difference. The next output&nbsp;
that we have are the dimensions of the object we&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:37.120 --> 01:34:42.960
are trying to detect and its Z coordinate&nbsp;
the distance or the height from the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.520 --> 01:34:52.400
In order to learn these four parameters, we use&nbsp;
what is called a balanced Elvan loss. Now given&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.400 --> 01:35:00.320
a training data, we can divide the points in that&nbsp;
data roughly into two categories, outliers and In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:00.320 --> 01:35:07.120
liars white training a network that data points&nbsp;
that generate a loss value that is less than some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:07.120 --> 01:35:15.440
threshold, let us say one are called in liars. And&nbsp;
the data points that have a loss that is greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:15.440 --> 01:35:23.760
than that threshold are called outliers. Due to a&nbsp;
larger value of loss, the outliers tend to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:24.400 --> 01:35:32.320
the neural network weights towards itself. Due to&nbsp;
a smaller loss value, the inliers are not able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:32.320 --> 01:35:39.200
attract the neural network weights towards itself&nbsp;
that sufficiently well. In order to efficiently&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.200 --> 01:35:45.200
deal with such an imbalance of loss values,&nbsp;
we use what is called a balanced elven loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:52.320
The balanced elven loss balances the loss values&nbsp;
that are generated by both of these inliers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:52.320 --> 01:35:59.280
outliers to get a network that performs well&nbsp;
on both these types of data. Alright, so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:59.280 --> 01:36:03.920
these are all the losses that are used in this&nbsp;
network. The next component that we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:03.920 --> 01:36:10.320
to discuss is the learning rate scheduling. While&nbsp;
training a neural network the learning rate is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:10.320 --> 01:36:16.240
important hyper parameter that we need to take&nbsp;
care of a particular technique that we apply to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:16.240 --> 01:36:22.320
better train a network is called learning rate&nbsp;
scheduling. During the training process, we tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:22.320 --> 01:36:28.880
to decrease or increase the learning rate values&nbsp;
in order to fine tune our optimization process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:29.680 --> 01:36:36.320
The learning rate decay technique that we&nbsp;
use in this network is the cosine annealing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:36.960 --> 01:36:43.920
the graph of the learning rate in cosine annealing&nbsp;
looks something like this towards the start&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:43.920 --> 01:36:49.680
the learning rate decreases slowly towards&nbsp;
the middle the learning rate starts decreasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:49.680 --> 01:36:55.680
in a linear fashion and towards the end, the&nbsp;
learning rate decrease again starts to slow down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:56.400 --> 01:37:02.400
The combination of all these three components&nbsp;
is what constitutes the SFA 3d technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:02.400 --> 01:37:07.840
alright. So, these are all the technical details&nbsp;
there are to discuss for the SFA 3d technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

