01:46:04.000 --> 01:46:10.880
architecture. Therefore, the unit access T has two&nbsp;
components the unit architecture itself and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:10.880 --> 01:46:17.200
extension let us discuss the two components one by&nbsp;
one. So, the first component is the unit network&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:18.000 --> 01:46:24.480
unit is a network that is used for semantic&nbsp;
segmentation. In the problem of semantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.480 --> 01:46:31.760
segmentation, we are required to segment a region&nbsp;
of interest from a given image. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:31.760 --> 01:46:37.840
in an image captured by a self driving car, we are&nbsp;
required to segment the cars that are present in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.840 --> 01:46:43.680
the image and the road that is present in the&nbsp;
image. We can do so, by highlighting them with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:43.680 --> 01:46:50.640
different colors. The unit architecture is similar&nbsp;
to other semantic segmentation architectures where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:50.640 --> 01:46:55.440
we have an encoder part of the network and&nbsp;
we have the decoder part of the network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.080 --> 01:47:01.280
The encoder part down samples the image&nbsp;
deriving different visual features in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:01.280 --> 01:47:07.840
image and then we have the decoder part which&nbsp;
up samples the image and returns us the output&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:08.560 --> 01:47:12.480
apart from the information derived&nbsp;
from the decoder part itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:12.480 --> 01:47:18.960
the decoder uses information from the encoder part&nbsp;
using skip connections as we can see over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:21.360 --> 01:47:27.920
Initially, unit architecture was devised&nbsp;
for biomedical image segmentation. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:27.920 --> 01:47:33.280
its use cases applies to other domains as well&nbsp;
for instance in our case self driving cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:34.240 --> 01:47:40.000
unit forms the basic architecture for this&nbsp;
technique. Now, let us discuss the Xs D are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:40.000 --> 01:47:46.480
the extension part of the unit network. The&nbsp;
extension part of the technique is that we make&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:46.480 --> 01:47:53.920
use of what are called spatial transformers. So,&nbsp;
before discussing what are spatial transformers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.920 --> 01:48:01.520
let us discuss the concept of homography. For&nbsp;
instance, we have an object and we take its image&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:01.520 --> 01:48:07.520
from a camera that is placed over here, we change&nbsp;
the camera position as well as its orientation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:07.520 --> 01:48:14.480
something over here. And we take the image again,&nbsp;
the transformation between these two images is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:14.480 --> 01:48:22.240
what is called homography. In mathematical terms,&nbsp;
homography is a simple matrix multiplication.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:22.880 --> 01:48:30.240
A cool application of mammography is that if&nbsp;
we know the homography matrix that converts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.240 --> 01:48:37.120
from one image plane to other image plane, then&nbsp;
from a single image, we can directly compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:37.120 --> 01:48:42.560
how that object would look like in a separate&nbsp;
image plane without even taking a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:42.560 --> 01:48:48.400
picture. However, in application, we would require&nbsp;
those image planes to be close to each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.960 --> 01:48:55.760
In general, the transformation need not be that&nbsp;
complex, they can be as simple as translating&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.760 --> 01:48:59.840
the camera plane or even rotating the&nbsp;
camera plane at a given fixed point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.800 --> 01:49:06.560
For instance, given this image, we are rotating&nbsp;
the image plane by approximately 15 degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.600 --> 01:49:13.840
Not theoretically, it would seem that we can&nbsp;
simply take an image x, multiply it by homography&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:13.840 --> 01:49:22.560
matrix H and get the final resulting image at x.&nbsp;
However, it is not that simple. In simple terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:22.560 --> 01:49:29.360
homography is a simple transformation that map's&nbsp;
pixels from one image to another image. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:29.360 --> 01:49:35.120
what we would have is this image pixel mapped&nbsp;
to some image pixel in this image over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:35.760 --> 01:49:43.280
On doing this matrix multiplication, it is easy&nbsp;
to observe that the output x x is not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:43.280 --> 01:49:50.880
output integer values. What this means is that a&nbsp;
given pixel over here is going to map to a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.880 --> 01:49:56.720
of different pixels over here. Hence, in order to&nbsp;
apply this transformation, we are going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:56.720 --> 01:50:02.720
a lot of different average calculations made&nbsp;
Making the final image look not really good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.360 --> 01:50:08.640
Hence, a solution to this is to do&nbsp;
an inverse homography calculation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:08.640 --> 01:50:13.920
what that means, instead of computing a&nbsp;
transformation that goes from input to output,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:13.920 --> 01:50:20.560
we instead calculate a transformation that&nbsp;
goes from the output pixel to the input pixels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:20.560 --> 01:50:27.680
For instance, this output pixel over here is going&nbsp;
to map to the input image in a rotated fashion&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.680 --> 01:50:34.960
something like this over here, we calculate an&nbsp;
average of the surrounding pixels in the input&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:34.960 --> 01:50:42.000
image. For instance, this pixel is going to map&nbsp;
to surrounding these four pixels over here. Hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:42.000 --> 01:50:48.160
we are going to calculate their weighted average&nbsp;
pixel values to derive the value of this pixel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:49.040 --> 01:50:54.880
This weighted average is calculated using&nbsp;
a technique called by linear interpolation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:55.920 --> 01:51:02.640
The weighted average is taken with respect to the&nbsp;
distance from the surrounding pixels. In place&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:02.640 --> 01:51:08.880
of by linear interpolation, we can apply other&nbsp;
different techniques like linear interpolation or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:08.880 --> 01:51:16.720
nearest neighbors as well. However, for spatial&nbsp;
transformers by linear interpolation is the choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.720 --> 01:51:23.520
due to its differentiated by nature. So, having&nbsp;
understood this complete process of homography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:24.240 --> 01:51:28.240
let us discuss the use of this&nbsp;
homography. In a spatial transformer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.960 --> 01:51:35.760
Spatial transformer is an extension module&nbsp;
that can be applied to any convolution network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:35.760 --> 01:51:42.800
This enables the convolution network to also learn&nbsp;
the different spatial properties that are present&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:42.800 --> 01:51:50.400
in the dataset. The spatial transformer learns&nbsp;
the different parameters of the homography metrics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.400 --> 01:51:57.280
in order to aid the convolution network. In the&nbsp;
same example, if we have an object over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:57.280 --> 01:52:04.160
and we take its image from this point, and let us&nbsp;
assume for some reason, the CNN architecture is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:04.160 --> 01:52:10.400
not able to classify this object accurately,&nbsp;
how spatial transformers are going to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:10.400 --> 01:52:15.360
the spatial transformers are going to&nbsp;
learn the homography matrix parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.360 --> 01:52:23.840
in such a way that they present an image to the&nbsp;
CNN from a different point, so that the CNN is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.840 --> 01:52:32.160
able to classify that object more accurately. So&nbsp;
having understood what is a spatial transformer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:32.160 --> 01:52:40.400
and how it helps, let us see how we apply this&nbsp;
to a unit xs D technique. For the unit accessory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:40.400 --> 01:52:44.560
technique, we are not going to learn&nbsp;
the parameters of the homography matrix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:45.200 --> 01:52:51.360
Instead, we are going to provide the spatial&nbsp;
transformers with a fixed homography matrix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:52.320 --> 01:52:58.640
So where do we get these homography parameters.&nbsp;
Since homography, is a general application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:58.640 --> 01:53:05.520
we can also apply this to our self driving cars&nbsp;
scenario as well. For instance, given this image&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:05.520 --> 01:53:12.640
from a right facing camera, we can easily derive&nbsp;
a homography that maps from this right camera to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:12.640 --> 01:53:20.080
a virtual camera that is present at the top. We&nbsp;
can do this for all the four cameras and derive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:20.080 --> 01:53:26.320
a top view of the environment. In this way,&nbsp;
we will easily get the homography parameters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:26.880 --> 01:53:33.040
This technique of deriving the top view from&nbsp;
these given images is also called inverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:33.040 --> 01:53:39.520
perspective mapping. But there's a natural&nbsp;
question to ask why we apply complex network&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:39.520 --> 01:53:45.520
architectures like unit access T, whereas we can&nbsp;
solve our problem using simple homography ease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:46.160 --> 01:53:52.960
The answer to this is the top view homography or&nbsp;
inverse perspective mapping makes an assumption&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:52.960 --> 01:53:59.520
